1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,880
An icon of architectural brilliance.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:08,920
Once the world's tallest building.

3
00:00:10,340 --> 00:00:12,460
Unmistakable on the New York skyline.

4
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:17,060
A tower of lights and stainless steel more than 1,000 feet high.

5
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:21,260
Now, the Chrysler Building on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,200
The Chrysler Building.

7
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:41,920
At 1,046 feet, it towers over one of the busiest intersections in midtown Manhattan.

8
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:46,280
It is one of the tallest buildings in the world

9
00:00:46,281 --> 00:00:50,120
and remains a favorite among residents and visitors alike.

10
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:56,300
There are many taller buildings, but it's the only one that starts out at the street

11
00:00:56,301 --> 00:00:58,960
and meets the sky with that kind of panache.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,180
I think it's probably the strongest punctuation in the skyline.

13
00:01:05,720 --> 00:01:09,580
Chrysler even once said he wanted people to walk into his building and marvel.

14
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:14,060
And that's why the design of it is so extravagant, so opulent.

15
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:22,360
Completed in 1930 at a cost of $15 million for auto tycoon Walter P. Chrysler,

16
00:01:23,260 --> 00:01:26,880
the Chrysler building was the tallest structure on Earth for 11 months

17
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:29,860
until the Empire State Building topped it.

18
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:31,500
by 204 feet.

19
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:37,300
The Chrysler building may no longer be the tallest,

20
00:01:37,301 --> 00:01:42,500
but what it lacks in height, it more than makes up for in style.

21
00:01:42,501 --> 00:01:45,680
Its lighted set of receding curves, eagles,

22
00:01:45,681 --> 00:01:49,060
and Chrysler hubcaps make the skyscraper different

23
00:01:49,061 --> 00:01:52,760
from anything built before or after.

24
00:01:52,761 --> 00:01:54,600
I'm a car buff.

25
00:01:54,601 --> 00:01:56,260
And that's the part of the building

26
00:01:56,261 --> 00:01:57,420
that brings it all together.

27
00:01:57,421 --> 00:01:58,400
It's the Chrysler building.

28
00:01:58,401 --> 00:01:59,840
And that's what Walter Chrysler did.

29
00:01:59,841 --> 00:02:01,040
He built automobiles.

30
00:02:02,740 --> 00:02:05,460
The radiator caps on the 31st floor

31
00:02:05,461 --> 00:02:09,020
were modeled from car ornaments on the 1926 Chrysler.

32
00:02:11,020 --> 00:02:14,700
But it's not just the automobile designs and eagles

33
00:02:14,701 --> 00:02:17,260
that make this skyscraper one of a kind.

34
00:02:17,940 --> 00:02:19,700
Inside, there are even more surprises.

35
00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:26,360
When you go into those dark black marble doors,

36
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:29,360
you find yourself in a triangular lobby.

37
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:33,480
where there are entrances and exits on several sides of the triangle.

38
00:02:34,140 --> 00:02:38,560
And you find yourself in a deeply atmospheric environment.

39
00:02:39,740 --> 00:02:44,460
The lobby is theatrically lit and decorated with stainless steel

40
00:02:44,461 --> 00:02:46,980
and granites and marbles from around the world.

41
00:02:47,720 --> 00:02:53,900
The lobby is just a work of art of the most exotic African tile

42
00:02:53,901 --> 00:02:56,520
and stainless steel and chandeliers

43
00:02:56,521 --> 00:02:59,960
and an early version of a digital clock.

44
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:02,200
Very space-age.

45
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:08,580
On the ceiling, a massive mural, 110 feet by 79 feet.

46
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:11,380
It's one of the largest murals in the country

47
00:03:11,381 --> 00:03:14,400
and features images, circa 1930,

48
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:17,840
of progress, transportation, and energy.

49
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:23,180
There's also a lavish marble staircase

50
00:03:23,181 --> 00:03:27,520
that leads to a shopping arcade and subways.

51
00:03:27,620 --> 00:03:31,620
Another unusual feature of the lobby are the elevators.

52
00:03:31,720 --> 00:03:34,920
There are 32 elevator cabs, each different,

53
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:38,220
each custom designed.

54
00:03:38,320 --> 00:03:41,040
One of the first things you'll notice

55
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:45,940
is the beautiful wood on the elevator cars.

56
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:47,740
There's American walnut.

57
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:50,380
There's Oriental walnut.

58
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:51,780
There's satin wood.

59
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:52,780
It's beautiful.

60
00:03:52,781 --> 00:03:54,180
It's absolutely fabulous.

61
00:03:56,980 --> 00:04:00,580
The elevators carry thousands of workers each morning

62
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:05,020
to 71 floors of offices.

63
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:08,420
More than 80 businesses call the Chrysler Building home,

64
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:10,800
ranging from financial firms to dental offices.

65
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:15,600
In the deeper reaches of the building

66
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:19,900
are the electrical systems and heating and cooling units.

67
00:04:19,901 --> 00:04:23,740
all have been updated to accommodate the demands of 21st century tenants

68
00:04:26,140 --> 00:04:31,580
but the building still retains vestiges of the decade it was born the roaring 20s

69
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:42,940
in the 1920s lots of things were happening because there was a business revival in europe

70
00:04:42,941 --> 00:04:48,140
and the united states after recovery from the first world war with the business revival you

71
00:04:48,141 --> 00:04:50,880
you could expect that there would be a need for new buildings.

72
00:04:52,880 --> 00:04:58,100
In the mid-1920s, plans were in the works for a skyscraper over 60 floors high

73
00:04:58,101 --> 00:05:00,980
at the corner of 42nd Street and Lexington Avenue,

74
00:05:01,980 --> 00:05:04,700
the future site of the Chrysler Building.

75
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:08,820
There was a project started by a guy named William H. Reynolds,

76
00:05:09,020 --> 00:05:12,100
who had no experience whatsoever in tall building construction.

77
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:16,220
I think he must have floated this idea simply to sell the project.

78
00:05:16,221 --> 00:05:19,160
He was an amusement park developer.

79
00:05:19,420 --> 00:05:23,220
He built a ride called Fire and Flames and The Last Days of Pompeii.

80
00:05:24,020 --> 00:05:25,800
So he definitely had a stunt mentality.

81
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:30,620
Reynolds hired New York architect William Van Allen,

82
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:35,080
who was known more for designing unusual shops and restaurants than anything else.

83
00:05:36,220 --> 00:05:42,020
To be perfectly honest, he was not regarded before the Chrysler Building

84
00:05:42,021 --> 00:05:45,640
as anything more than simply a clever designer.

85
00:05:46,220 --> 00:05:51,220
Furthermore, Van Allen had never built anything taller than a few stories.

86
00:05:51,221 --> 00:05:54,220
Yet he seized this project in earnest.

87
00:05:54,221 --> 00:06:00,220
Van Allen had prepared a scheme for the building, which didn't have that signature top to it,

88
00:06:00,221 --> 00:06:06,220
but it already had that patterning, this amazing contrast of horizontal and vertical

89
00:06:06,221 --> 00:06:13,220
on the lower floors like a basket weave, and then the upper floors with all those indentations.

90
00:06:13,221 --> 00:06:17,360
There was a reason for the indentations and setbacks.

91
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:20,680
New York City's 1916 zoning law.

92
00:06:22,020 --> 00:06:25,640
Buildings were allowed now to rise only up to a certain height,

93
00:06:25,641 --> 00:06:29,500
and then they had to set back in a series of steps,

94
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:33,840
like a sort of wedding cake tower or boxes piled one on top of the other,

95
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,260
in order to allow for light to come down into the streets.

96
00:06:38,261 --> 00:06:44,260
Van Allen was also limited by standard building conventions.

97
00:06:44,261 --> 00:06:50,980
In a time before air conditioning and fluorescent lighting, workers needed access to fresh air

98
00:06:50,981 --> 00:06:51,807
and light.

99
00:06:51,808 --> 00:06:58,420
Van Allen placed all offices on the perimeter near the windows and all elevators

100
00:06:58,421 --> 00:07:00,260
and utility areas in the center.

101
00:07:00,261 --> 00:07:05,940
He had drawn countless sketches, but they were going nowhere. Imagine what it's like

102
00:07:05,941 --> 00:07:11,920
to be an architect who is drawing these wonderful designs

103
00:07:11,921 --> 00:07:13,780
for the great building that's going

104
00:07:13,781 --> 00:07:17,080
to be the pinnacle of his career, and yet there's

105
00:07:17,081 --> 00:07:18,660
no money to build it.

106
00:07:18,661 --> 00:07:22,140
And to know that William Reynolds probably

107
00:07:22,141 --> 00:07:25,880
had no intention of actually building it.

108
00:07:25,881 --> 00:07:30,400
Then in 1928, automobile tycoon Walter P. Chrysler

109
00:07:30,401 --> 00:07:32,880
decided he wanted to build an office building in New York

110
00:07:32,881 --> 00:07:35,280
City.

111
00:07:35,281 --> 00:07:37,980
He struck a deal with Reynolds for $2.5 million

112
00:07:37,981 --> 00:07:40,920
and acquired the skyscraper project,

113
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:42,640
including Van Allen's designs.

114
00:07:44,100 --> 00:07:47,320
Once Reynolds released the project to Chrysler,

115
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:49,700
Van Allen must have been the happiest man in the world.

116
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:53,860
Walter Chrysler came from modest means.

117
00:07:54,820 --> 00:07:56,020
Born and raised in Kansas,

118
00:07:56,360 --> 00:07:58,600
he became a millionaire in the 1910s

119
00:07:58,601 --> 00:08:00,660
through salary and stock options

120
00:08:00,661 --> 00:08:03,420
earned as a vice president for General Motors Corporation.

121
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:12,480
By 1925, Chrysler was running his own car company and earning more millions from a car bearing his name.

122
00:08:14,220 --> 00:08:16,320
The car was an immediate hit.

123
00:08:16,660 --> 00:08:25,620
They sold 32,000 in the first year, which was, up until that moment, the all-time record for a new model introduction.

124
00:08:26,300 --> 00:08:27,920
It was the car everybody wanted.

125
00:08:28,980 --> 00:08:30,420
Chrysler was on a roll.

126
00:08:31,040 --> 00:08:34,460
Although he didn't have any more experience building skyscrapers than Reynolds,

127
00:08:34,461 --> 00:08:37,360
He recognized a good location when he saw it.

128
00:08:37,361 --> 00:08:41,760
His new office building would be steps away from Grand Central Station.

129
00:08:41,761 --> 00:08:46,840
If you're right on top of a railroad terminal, you've got a prime location.

130
00:08:46,841 --> 00:08:50,500
So it was very easy for the boss to live in Scarsdale or Bronxville,

131
00:08:50,501 --> 00:08:54,080
and he would commute right into Grand Central Terminal.

132
00:08:54,081 --> 00:09:01,060
Chrysler asked Van Allen to design a building that was taller than the Woolworth building,

133
00:09:01,061 --> 00:09:07,060
which at 792 feet had been the world's tallest skyscraper since 1913.

134
00:09:10,060 --> 00:09:14,820
Chrysler tells Van Allen, I want you to build the tallest building in the world.

135
00:09:15,100 --> 00:09:19,200
I want it to be of the finest character. I want you to spend what it takes.

136
00:09:19,660 --> 00:09:22,900
He was basically given a license to create his masterpiece.

137
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:28,160
But Chrysler and Van Allen were about to have competition.

138
00:09:29,140 --> 00:09:36,060
Downtown in the Wall Street District, the Bank of Manhattan Company announced it was going to build a skyscraper at 40 Wall Street

139
00:09:36,061 --> 00:09:38,840
that was going to be even taller than the Chrysler Building.

140
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:45,960
The race to build the world's tallest skyscraper begins in startling fashion,

141
00:09:46,360 --> 00:09:51,400
as half a dozen steam shovels and an army of men break ground for the new Chrysler Building.

142
00:09:51,401 --> 00:09:59,500
In 1928, a race was on in New York City to create the world's tallest building.

143
00:10:00,580 --> 00:10:05,860
Backers of the Bank of Manhattan skyscraper at 40 Wall Street started construction early that year.

144
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:10,660
Meanwhile, the Chrysler Building was still a concept on paper,

145
00:10:11,100 --> 00:10:13,980
as architect William Van Allen went back to do some revisions.

146
00:10:15,080 --> 00:10:20,460
With the skyscraper race, he was forced to go back again to the drawing board

147
00:10:20,461 --> 00:10:24,660
and find a way to terminate the dome.

148
00:10:24,980 --> 00:10:26,740
And so what you find is that the dome,

149
00:10:26,741 --> 00:10:29,880
which would have been a little bit stunted at its top,

150
00:10:30,480 --> 00:10:33,740
became increasingly smaller each step of the way

151
00:10:33,741 --> 00:10:37,800
until it ended in the Great Vertex, as they know it.

152
00:10:39,460 --> 00:10:42,400
Construction on the Chrysler Building started in the fall.

153
00:10:43,660 --> 00:10:45,480
There were old basements on the site.

154
00:10:46,120 --> 00:10:48,180
Every tall building in New York was built someplace

155
00:10:48,181 --> 00:10:49,900
where there used to be small buildings.

156
00:10:50,460 --> 00:10:53,300
So they had to get below that level to even start.

157
00:10:53,301 --> 00:10:56,200
And then from there, they would go down to bedrock.

158
00:10:56,201 --> 00:10:59,900
But using dynamite to clear the site was too risky.

159
00:10:59,901 --> 00:11:01,600
Blasting is not that popular in Manhattan

160
00:11:01,601 --> 00:11:03,900
because of the fact there's always adjacent buildings.

161
00:11:03,901 --> 00:11:05,700
But you absolutely can't blast when

162
00:11:05,701 --> 00:11:09,520
you're near two subway lines and immediately adjacent

163
00:11:09,521 --> 00:11:14,160
to a number of small 19th century buildings.

164
00:11:14,161 --> 00:11:17,160
Workers instead used brute strength and jackhammers

165
00:11:17,161 --> 00:11:20,460
powered by steam compressors.

166
00:11:20,461 --> 00:11:25,420
More than 50,000 cubic yards of soil and rock were then removed by steam shovels,

167
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,160
the forerunners of modern backhoes.

168
00:11:31,100 --> 00:11:34,160
Every one of those steam engines has to release excess steam,

169
00:11:34,560 --> 00:11:36,140
and you have that whistle when it does that.

170
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:38,300
So you would have had an enormous amount of noise

171
00:11:38,301 --> 00:11:40,460
from all of these machines wandering around the site,

172
00:11:40,820 --> 00:11:44,200
not to mention men hammering into rock with compressed air.

173
00:11:44,900 --> 00:11:47,560
The noise didn't keep away a crowd of gawkers.

174
00:11:47,561 --> 00:11:52,960
There's a perimeter of people, spectators, no fences.

175
00:11:53,700 --> 00:11:56,100
And everybody's just watching this thing happen,

176
00:11:56,160 --> 00:11:58,820
as if you could just kind of walk down there and ask the guy,

177
00:11:59,820 --> 00:12:02,100
you know, how's everything going, Joe?

178
00:12:03,800 --> 00:12:07,520
A month after excavation of the site, foundation work began.

179
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:12,020
The first step was to create steel and concrete footings for the building.

180
00:12:13,160 --> 00:12:15,960
Nowadays, we would create a footing out of reinforced concrete

181
00:12:15,961 --> 00:12:18,380
where we put rebar into the concrete,

182
00:12:18,500 --> 00:12:19,500
and the two work together.

183
00:12:20,500 --> 00:12:22,060
The foundations of the Chrysler building

184
00:12:22,061 --> 00:12:24,060
are what's called grillage foundations.

185
00:12:25,260 --> 00:12:28,240
Workers placed sets of steel I-beams close together

186
00:12:28,241 --> 00:12:29,740
so that they almost touch.

187
00:12:31,240 --> 00:12:33,900
Enough of them were used to create a kind of box

188
00:12:33,901 --> 00:12:35,760
that was 10 to 12 feet square.

189
00:12:36,580 --> 00:12:38,880
On top of this was a second layer of beams.

190
00:12:39,540 --> 00:12:42,080
A final steel column was placed on top of that.

191
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:44,640
So if you picture the load coming down from the column,

192
00:12:44,641 --> 00:12:50,460
that first beam spreads it out, and then the second, the large group of beams spreads it out in the other direction.

193
00:12:52,300 --> 00:12:55,860
Workers poured concrete over the steel to waterproof and protect it.

194
00:12:57,560 --> 00:13:04,200
Each footing would weigh more than 35 tons and would be capable of supporting 10 times its own weight.

195
00:13:06,660 --> 00:13:11,280
In the 1920s, buildings standing higher than 50 stories were still a novelty,

196
00:13:11,281 --> 00:13:14,320
but the means to construct them were not.

197
00:13:14,640 --> 00:13:21,020
The technology that made skyscrapers possible dated back to 19th century innovations.

198
00:13:22,020 --> 00:13:23,140
The first was the elevator.

199
00:13:25,920 --> 00:13:30,640
The height of buildings was once limited to four or five stories, or the number of stairs

200
00:13:30,641 --> 00:13:32,180
a person could comfortably climb.

201
00:13:34,640 --> 00:13:40,760
Then in 1853, Elijah Otis perfected the passenger elevator system.

202
00:13:40,761 --> 00:13:48,480
It was shown at the Crystal Palace Exhibition here in New York City in 1853 when Elijah

203
00:13:48,481 --> 00:13:53,820
Gray Otis cut the safety cord and said, you know, voila, he was safe.

204
00:13:53,821 --> 00:13:58,460
And that was to reassure people that they could ride this mechanical contraption to

205
00:13:58,461 --> 00:14:01,460
an upper floor.

206
00:14:01,461 --> 00:14:06,740
Prior to this, elevators were no more than lifts supported by cables and hoisted by manpower

207
00:14:06,741 --> 00:14:10,000
or mechanical pulleys.

208
00:14:10,001 --> 00:14:15,402
it would take a good 20 years before the passenger elevator was fully developed.

209
00:14:15,403 --> 00:14:16,280
By the 1920s,

210
00:14:16,281 --> 00:14:20,680
cars were enclosed and cables attached to the car were looped around a pulley called

211
00:14:20,681 --> 00:14:21,381
a sheave.

212
00:14:21,382 --> 00:14:27,840
The sheave was connected to a motor which would either raise or lower the elevator.

213
00:14:27,841 --> 00:14:31,800
Steel frame construction was the next innovation that made skyscrapers like the Chrysler Building

214
00:14:31,801 --> 00:14:37,580
possible. Prior to the steel frame, masonry construction

215
00:14:37,581 --> 00:14:41,120
was used for commercial buildings.

216
00:14:41,121 --> 00:14:44,800
However, a major drawback was that the taller the structure,

217
00:14:44,801 --> 00:14:48,440
the thicker the walls were to support it.

218
00:14:48,441 --> 00:14:51,440
In 1885, the Home Insurance Building in Chicago

219
00:14:51,441 --> 00:14:54,300
was the first tall building to use the structural steel

220
00:14:54,301 --> 00:14:57,000
frame.

221
00:14:57,001 --> 00:15:00,300
More importantly, steel columns riveted to steel beams

222
00:15:00,301 --> 00:15:03,340
could be stacked vertically, providing a stable structure

223
00:15:03,341 --> 00:15:05,720
for dozens of floors.

224
00:15:05,721 --> 00:15:13,820
The thick masonry walls that once supported buildings gave way to thin veneer or curtain walls of brick, stone, or even glass.

225
00:15:14,820 --> 00:15:20,000
More square footage than was previously dreamed possible could be built on commercial sites.

226
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:25,900
By the 1920s, the steel frame, the elevator, and the concentration of business districts

227
00:15:25,901 --> 00:15:32,140
meant great upward pressure for real estate developers to build higher and higher.

228
00:15:32,141 --> 00:15:39,480
How effective steel frame construction would be was about to be put to the test in the spring of 1929,

229
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:43,100
when the first steel columns of the Chrysler building went up.

230
00:15:43,920 --> 00:15:45,980
You put your new columns in place.

231
00:15:46,140 --> 00:15:49,500
Now they're sticking up two stories on top of whatever it is you've had up to that date.

232
00:15:50,060 --> 00:15:51,840
And then you start putting in the beams to connect them.

233
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:53,680
And this process just keeps repeating.

234
00:15:55,260 --> 00:15:58,240
Steel beams were carried upward by large cranes.

235
00:15:58,241 --> 00:16:02,560
This type of crane is nothing but the two boom arms and a lot of cables.

236
00:16:03,140 --> 00:16:05,820
Every time you need to push the crane up, you can take the crane apart,

237
00:16:07,160 --> 00:16:09,380
reassemble it in a new location in a matter of hours,

238
00:16:09,460 --> 00:16:11,400
and then work on the next tier of steel.

239
00:16:12,300 --> 00:16:16,160
Once columns were erected, they were connected to beams by steel rivets.

240
00:16:17,400 --> 00:16:21,740
Rivets are small cylinders of steel with no threads and one round head.

241
00:16:22,980 --> 00:16:26,780
They're heated, red-hot, on-site in what's called a salamander,

242
00:16:26,781 --> 00:16:28,980
or a little coal-filled bucket.

243
00:16:28,981 --> 00:16:32,080
As the rivets are needed, the man at the salamander

244
00:16:32,081 --> 00:16:35,860
would pick them up, toss them, and one of the rivet gangs

245
00:16:35,861 --> 00:16:37,200
sitting next to where the work's going on

246
00:16:37,201 --> 00:16:42,120
would have a tin funnel to catch them.

247
00:16:42,121 --> 00:16:45,400
Workers would place them in holes between joints.

248
00:16:45,401 --> 00:16:47,300
One man would hold the back in place,

249
00:16:47,301 --> 00:16:49,380
while another, with a pneumatic hammer,

250
00:16:49,381 --> 00:16:53,400
would pound down the cylinder until it formed a round head.

251
00:16:53,401 --> 00:16:55,040
As it cools, it shrinks.

252
00:16:55,041 --> 00:16:58,100
So it clamps the two pieces of steel together as it shrinks.

253
00:16:58,480 --> 00:17:01,020
It's a fairly effective way of making connections.

254
00:17:02,700 --> 00:17:05,320
But as the floors of the Chrysler building rose,

255
00:17:05,720 --> 00:17:08,000
so did the need for a construction elevator.

256
00:17:09,600 --> 00:17:12,940
If you made a man begin his day by climbing 40 flights of stairs,

257
00:17:13,040 --> 00:17:14,320
he wasn't going to be very productive

258
00:17:14,321 --> 00:17:16,360
compared to if you gave him an elevator ride up.

259
00:17:17,580 --> 00:17:19,620
With the addition of construction elevators

260
00:17:19,621 --> 00:17:21,380
on the outside of the Chrysler building,

261
00:17:21,840 --> 00:17:23,980
work continued at a breakneck pace.

262
00:17:25,040 --> 00:17:28,560
Floors were going up at a record speed of four per week.

263
00:17:29,820 --> 00:17:35,000
But downtown, Walter P. Chrysler's quest to build the world's tallest skyscraper

264
00:17:35,001 --> 00:17:40,040
was still threatened by the soon-to-be-completed Bank of Manhattan building at 40 Wall Street.

265
00:17:40,900 --> 00:17:44,020
The 40 Wall Street team who backed the Bank of Manhattan building

266
00:17:44,021 --> 00:17:47,180
announced that they were going to go 47 stories tall.

267
00:17:48,020 --> 00:17:53,780
The Chrysler building has announced that it's going to be 808 feet tall, 68 stories.

268
00:17:54,640 --> 00:17:59,900
Several weeks later, it's announced that the Bank of Manhattan building is going to be 63 stories,

269
00:18:00,020 --> 00:18:02,060
and it's going to be 840 feet tall.

270
00:18:05,640 --> 00:18:11,680
By mid-summer 1929, the Chrysler Building was near the halfway point of its construction,

271
00:18:12,220 --> 00:18:17,800
and people who passed 42nd and Lexington could begin to see that this was no ordinary skyscraper.

272
00:18:18,680 --> 00:18:23,660
It wasn't just the size that made people look upward, but also its intricate design.

273
00:18:23,780 --> 00:18:30,340
You see the tire, you see the bumper and the wheel well, and there's multiples of them.

274
00:18:30,700 --> 00:18:34,360
It actually looked like a wave pattern that you would see in more traditional architecture

275
00:18:34,361 --> 00:18:37,860
and some of the moldings that you see a wave pattern in plaster work.

276
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:41,980
I mean, that's an immensely difficult task right there, is to take an architectural drawing

277
00:18:41,981 --> 00:18:46,940
that shows this is the way I want the building striped and turn that into bricks out in the

278
00:18:46,941 --> 00:18:47,641
field.

279
00:18:47,660 --> 00:18:49,660
And to a large extent, it was the Masons who did that.

280
00:18:49,661 --> 00:18:56,660
The innovative designs were the result of close collaboration between Van Allen and his client, Walter P.

281
00:18:56,661 --> 00:18:57,361
Chrysler.

282
00:18:58,180 --> 00:19:01,480
The two worked together extremely well.

283
00:19:01,800 --> 00:19:04,960
Chrysler noted a number of times how much fun he was having,

284
00:19:05,560 --> 00:19:08,960
going over the designs and plaster models with Van Allen.

285
00:19:09,880 --> 00:19:12,020
As the walls of the Chrysler building went up,

286
00:19:12,840 --> 00:19:17,060
more than 3,800 windows with galvanized steel frames followed.

287
00:19:17,061 --> 00:19:20,060
Once you have a floor where the windows are in place,

288
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:23,060
that floor is enclosed, and now all the interior trades

289
00:19:23,160 --> 00:19:24,060
can start to work.

290
00:19:24,160 --> 00:19:27,060
You can have electricians, plumbers, plasterers work

291
00:19:27,160 --> 00:19:30,060
on the inside because it's now enclosed against the weather.

292
00:19:33,160 --> 00:19:36,060
In the meantime, almost 1,000 feet off the ground,

293
00:19:36,160 --> 00:19:39,060
scaffolding was being assembled for work on the Chrysler

294
00:19:39,160 --> 00:19:40,060
Building dome.

295
00:19:42,160 --> 00:19:44,060
What they did was they used pipe scaffolding,

296
00:19:44,160 --> 00:19:46,060
which is very adjustable.

297
00:19:46,061 --> 00:19:47,560
You have pipes in three dimensions.

298
00:19:47,561 --> 00:19:49,400
You have vertical pipes, you have pipes this way,

299
00:19:49,401 --> 00:19:50,400
you have pipes this way.

300
00:19:50,401 --> 00:19:52,400
Then you have diagonals to brace this.

301
00:19:54,400 --> 00:19:55,900
The skeletal structure of the dome

302
00:19:55,901 --> 00:19:58,900
was made of curved beams of steel.

303
00:19:58,901 --> 00:20:01,600
Its interior walls were brick, but the dome itself

304
00:20:01,601 --> 00:20:06,220
was covered with a stainless steel material called nyrosta.

305
00:20:06,221 --> 00:20:11,260
No one in the United States had ever used it for this purpose.

306
00:20:11,261 --> 00:20:13,220
It was Chrysler who chose this steel,

307
00:20:13,221 --> 00:20:17,220
this steel which came from the Krupp Metalworks in Germany.

308
00:20:17,221 --> 00:20:22,220
Chrysler had it tested in his laboratories and saw that it had wonderful properties.

309
00:20:22,221 --> 00:20:30,220
It did not rust, it was uniform in quality throughout, it never had to be maintained in any sort of a way.

310
00:20:32,220 --> 00:20:38,220
Stainless steel is any steel alloy that has 12% or more chromium, so as to be rust resistant.

311
00:20:38,221 --> 00:20:48,220
Nyrosta was made up of steel including 18% chromium and 8% nickel.

312
00:20:48,221 --> 00:20:54,220
Metal workers measured and cut sheets in a metal shop set up inside the building.

313
00:20:54,221 --> 00:20:58,220
Roofers then soldered and crimped the sheets into place.

314
00:20:58,221 --> 00:21:01,220
It was all custom hand fabricated work.

315
00:21:01,221 --> 00:21:12,080
40 tons of nairosta steel were used on the roof and extensively on other parts of the building, including the eagles, wings of mercury radiator caps, and pineapples.

316
00:21:13,940 --> 00:21:17,715
The eagles, however, are among the most striking elements of the building.

317
00:21:17,716 --> 00:21:19,960
There are eight of them, two on each corner.

318
00:21:21,820 --> 00:21:29,840
A friend of Van Allen's and top architectural renderer of the period, Chesley Bonestell, is credited with having designed the eagles.

319
00:21:31,220 --> 00:21:35,220
Bonasel himself wasn't really an architect.

320
00:21:35,221 --> 00:21:40,220
He was an architectural renderer, which was a person who takes an architect's plans and

321
00:21:40,221 --> 00:21:46,220
turns them into a painting that shows what the building will look like when it's finished.

322
00:21:47,220 --> 00:21:52,220
Off-site, the Eagles were carefully sculptured with sheets of Nyrosta.

323
00:21:52,221 --> 00:21:57,220
They were then fitted onto iron frames protruding from the building.

324
00:21:57,221 --> 00:22:00,220
During the installation, no one treated them with kid gloves.

325
00:22:00,221 --> 00:22:03,220
All right, boys, the eagle's right now.

326
00:22:03,221 --> 00:22:04,220
She can't fly off.

327
00:22:04,221 --> 00:22:05,220
He's all right.

328
00:22:05,221 --> 00:22:07,220
She can't fly off of it.

329
00:22:07,221 --> 00:22:09,220
Every time they hit it, they leave another little dent

330
00:22:09,221 --> 00:22:10,220
in the stainless steel.

331
00:22:10,221 --> 00:22:11,220
And you're thinking to yourself, my God,

332
00:22:11,221 --> 00:22:12,220
this is going to look terrible.

333
00:22:12,221 --> 00:22:15,220
But again, people see it from 800 feet away.

334
00:22:15,221 --> 00:22:17,220
It looks great.

335
00:22:17,221 --> 00:22:20,220
As autumn fell in 1929, the Chrysler building

336
00:22:20,221 --> 00:22:24,220
was standing more than 70 stories tall.

337
00:22:24,221 --> 00:22:28,220
The steel work and brick work was almost done.

338
00:22:28,221 --> 00:22:34,160
However, the Bank of Manhattan, the Chrysler Building rival at 40 Wall Street, was nearer

339
00:22:34,161 --> 00:22:40,900
to completion and was well on its way to capturing the title of world's tallest building.

340
00:22:40,901 --> 00:22:47,060
The Bank of Manhattan again hears rumors that there's actually a 60-foot flagpole going

341
00:22:47,061 --> 00:22:48,880
up on the Chrysler Building.

342
00:22:48,881 --> 00:22:54,120
So they make additional changes to carry their building to 925 feet.

343
00:22:54,121 --> 00:22:56,760
The builders at 40 Wall Street responded to the rumor

344
00:22:56,761 --> 00:23:00,020
by slapping on a flagpole of their own.

345
00:23:00,021 --> 00:23:01,860
But unbeknownst to their rivals,

346
00:23:01,861 --> 00:23:04,740
Chrysler and Van Allen had been working on a secret scheme

347
00:23:04,741 --> 00:23:06,140
they cooked up months before.

348
00:23:08,900 --> 00:23:13,200
In June 1929, Van Allen comes up with the idea

349
00:23:13,201 --> 00:23:18,740
for this great vertex, this great slender pyramid

350
00:23:18,741 --> 00:23:21,380
that was 185 feet tall.

351
00:23:21,381 --> 00:23:24,080
The problem was how to lift it atop the building

352
00:23:24,081 --> 00:23:27,620
without it falling over the side and crushing automobiles

353
00:23:27,621 --> 00:23:30,080
and risking the lives of the people on the street.

354
00:23:32,360 --> 00:23:35,300
The solution was to build the Vertex in secret

355
00:23:35,301 --> 00:23:37,420
at the very top of the Chrysler Building

356
00:23:37,421 --> 00:23:39,080
inside the fire tower.

357
00:23:41,200 --> 00:23:44,400
The Bank of Manhattan knows nothing

358
00:23:44,401 --> 00:23:47,800
of Chrysler and Van Allen's plan.

359
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:50,700
Then in the summer, they hear a rumor

360
00:23:50,701 --> 00:23:52,440
that the Chrysler Building is not actually

361
00:23:52,441 --> 00:23:56,026
to be 808 feet. They're going taller.

362
00:23:56,027 --> 00:24:02,520
On October 23rd, 1929, a derrick was rigged at the top of the dome.

363
00:24:03,960 --> 00:24:08,840
Workers attached the 27-ton spire and hoisted it up and out the top of the building.

364
00:24:10,680 --> 00:24:12,352
The job took 90 minutes.

365
00:24:12,353 --> 00:24:16,600
To bring the spire on top would have been much more difficult

366
00:24:16,601 --> 00:24:22,340
to constructing the spire and then jacking it up through and to the top and then fastening it.

367
00:24:22,640 --> 00:24:24,700
So it actually made great construction logic.

368
00:24:25,320 --> 00:24:32,320
But it was quite startling to have the building overnight, as it were, just grow this giant mast.

369
00:24:34,220 --> 00:24:38,620
On a clear fall day, the Chrysler building became the tallest in the world

370
00:24:38,621 --> 00:24:42,740
at 1,046 feet and 4 and 3 quarter inches.

371
00:24:42,741 --> 00:24:55,380
It was taller than the 792-foot Woolworth Building, taller than the 984-foot Eiffel Tower, and most importantly, taller than 40 Wall Street.

372
00:24:55,980 --> 00:24:57,420
It was a stunning achievement.

373
00:24:58,420 --> 00:25:04,260
Not one newspaper wrote anything about it. It was never mentioned.

374
00:25:04,261 --> 00:25:08,900
It could have been the fact that one day after they raised the spire,

375
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:11,540
this great crowning achievement of the Roaring Twenties,

376
00:25:11,640 --> 00:25:16,240
where America finally eclipses the French in having the tallest building in the world.

377
00:25:17,040 --> 00:25:21,160
The stock market crashes, and it's devastation on Wall Street.

378
00:25:21,380 --> 00:25:24,520
People were looking at their ticker tapes and not towards the sky.

379
00:25:28,080 --> 00:25:31,380
Chrysler's claim to the world's tallest building was short-lived.

380
00:25:31,381 --> 00:25:34,080
In less than a year, the Empire State Building

381
00:25:34,180 --> 00:25:37,280
would wrestle the title away at 1,250 feet.

382
00:25:39,980 --> 00:25:42,120
And while the Empire State Building may have ended up

383
00:25:42,220 --> 00:25:44,620
taller than the Chrysler Building,

384
00:25:44,720 --> 00:25:48,060
it was no match to the stainless steel capped skyscraper

385
00:25:48,160 --> 00:25:49,460
when it came to mystery.

386
00:25:52,100 --> 00:25:55,240
Chrysler keeps some of the best and most extravagant floors

387
00:25:55,340 --> 00:25:58,000
of his building behind closed doors

388
00:25:58,100 --> 00:26:01,380
and out of public view.

389
00:26:01,381 --> 00:26:06,580
Construction of the world-famous Chrysler Building finished in a record 18 months.

390
00:26:07,620 --> 00:26:10,460
During that time, no fatalities were reported.

391
00:26:11,500 --> 00:26:13,800
The safety record of the building was extraordinary.

392
00:26:14,620 --> 00:26:23,200
In those days, it was considered the norm for a building to have one death for every floor above the 15th floor.

393
00:26:23,480 --> 00:26:28,249
And this was a 77-story building, so by rights, it should have had 62 deaths.

394
00:26:28,250 --> 00:26:29,240
There were none.

395
00:26:31,380 --> 00:26:33,980
It was an amazing safety record attributed

396
00:26:33,981 --> 00:26:37,600
to the efficiency of the work site.

397
00:26:37,601 --> 00:26:40,580
It was a lot of professionalism being developed of this

398
00:26:40,581 --> 00:26:42,480
is how you do things.

399
00:26:42,481 --> 00:26:45,840
And as that happened, the number of workers who

400
00:26:45,841 --> 00:26:47,580
got killed per building dropped.

401
00:26:50,320 --> 00:26:52,080
In the construction of the Chrysler building,

402
00:26:52,081 --> 00:26:58,380
more than 20,000 tons of structural steel were used,

403
00:26:58,381 --> 00:27:02,220
More than 300,000 rivets, 3 million bricks,

404
00:27:02,221 --> 00:27:04,560
and 10,000 light bulbs.

405
00:27:04,561 --> 00:27:12,900
3,862 windows were hung and 2,788 doors.

406
00:27:12,901 --> 00:27:16,120
In addition, 35 miles of pipes were connected

407
00:27:16,121 --> 00:27:19,600
and 750 miles of electrical wire installed.

408
00:27:22,560 --> 00:27:24,720
The Chrysler Building officially opened its doors

409
00:27:24,721 --> 00:27:28,140
to the public on May 27, 1930.

410
00:27:28,141 --> 00:27:30,640
reactions were mixed.

411
00:27:30,641 --> 00:27:34,900
When the Chrysler Building was finished in 1930,

412
00:27:34,901 --> 00:27:38,380
traditional architectural critics considered it a stunt.

413
00:27:38,381 --> 00:27:41,700
Some people were ashamed of it and thought it was,

414
00:27:41,701 --> 00:27:46,200
you know, an indulgence and an excess and somehow, you know,

415
00:27:46,201 --> 00:27:47,780
off the wall.

416
00:27:47,781 --> 00:27:49,700
Of course, I think it's exactly the building that

417
00:27:49,701 --> 00:27:51,480
should be on 42nd Street.

418
00:27:51,481 --> 00:27:55,540
I can't imagine a better location than on that

419
00:27:55,541 --> 00:27:56,540
wonderful thoroughfare.

420
00:27:58,140 --> 00:28:03,380
Whether the critics loved or hated it was of little concern to managers of the skyscraper.

421
00:28:04,660 --> 00:28:07,820
The Chrysler Building had a number of rent-paying tenants.

422
00:28:08,760 --> 00:28:12,020
The Empire State Building, when it finally opened, did not.

423
00:28:12,420 --> 00:28:14,640
No, the empty state building is what it was called.

424
00:28:14,960 --> 00:28:17,120
The Empire State Building is not in a great location.

425
00:28:17,440 --> 00:28:20,060
Neither was Rockefeller Center. They weren't right on top of the railroads.

426
00:28:21,780 --> 00:28:25,920
Chrysler also managed to sign up tenants before the Depression hit full force.

427
00:28:25,921 --> 00:28:30,120
The Texaco Corporation leased a quarter of the building.

428
00:28:30,121 --> 00:28:35,280
The Chrysler Corporation had offices and a showroom,

429
00:28:35,281 --> 00:28:37,740
featuring the latest Chrysler automobiles.

430
00:28:37,741 --> 00:28:42,600
Tenants and their visitors enjoyed state-of-the-art conveniences

431
00:28:42,601 --> 00:28:44,100
and a taste of luxury.

432
00:28:44,101 --> 00:28:47,940
Everything was minutely designed.

433
00:28:47,941 --> 00:28:52,440
And it was important for people's first impression

434
00:28:52,441 --> 00:28:54,360
to be one of grandeur.

435
00:28:54,361 --> 00:28:56,360
And that's what they got.

436
00:28:58,100 --> 00:29:02,020
The lobby of the building featured a stylish restaurant and coffee shop.

437
00:29:05,120 --> 00:29:07,720
The elevators were the fastest in the world,

438
00:29:08,340 --> 00:29:12,340
capable of ascending at an ear-popping 1,000 feet per minute.

439
00:29:14,120 --> 00:29:18,240
They were very proud of the speed of the elevators.

440
00:29:18,241 --> 00:29:22,340
They were very proud of the ventilation systems,

441
00:29:22,341 --> 00:29:29,200
and they believed that this was the most efficient, up-to-date kind of working environment that could be imagined.

442
00:29:30,300 --> 00:29:33,920
Chrysler spared nothing about making it a desirable address.

443
00:29:35,800 --> 00:29:42,640
On the 71st floor, up beyond the offices in Chrysler's stainless steel tower, was an observatory.

444
00:29:45,640 --> 00:29:50,460
Tourists could look out of triangular-shaped windows for views of Manhattan and beyond,

445
00:29:50,461 --> 00:29:53,600
But the views weren't the only thing to gaze at.

446
00:29:54,320 --> 00:29:58,720
The observatory had strange slanted walls that were painted with stars and planets,

447
00:29:59,160 --> 00:30:01,340
and light fixtures that resembled Saturn.

448
00:30:04,020 --> 00:30:07,140
Tourists could also peer into Walter Chrysler's old toolbox

449
00:30:07,141 --> 00:30:10,940
and see the tools he had made by hand as a young mechanic.

450
00:30:11,600 --> 00:30:14,220
And when he opened the building, he put those tools there.

451
00:30:14,540 --> 00:30:17,640
And he said, you can stand on this observation platform

452
00:30:17,641 --> 00:30:21,400
and see 40 miles in all directions and learn a great deal about America.

453
00:30:21,660 --> 00:30:25,820
But you can learn a great deal more about America by gazing into that toolbox.

454
00:30:28,980 --> 00:30:32,120
Mystery shrouded other floors in the Chrysler building tower.

455
00:30:33,300 --> 00:30:38,220
It was invitation only to Walter P. Chrysler's private office on the 65th floor.

456
00:30:40,060 --> 00:30:43,000
He had a lot of carved wood.

457
00:30:43,100 --> 00:30:46,280
All of his furniture was custom made.

458
00:30:46,281 --> 00:30:47,740
Very nice office.

459
00:30:48,980 --> 00:30:55,800
On floors 66 through 68 was an exclusive men's luncheon club called the Cloud Club.

460
00:30:56,960 --> 00:30:59,869
Captains of industry like Chrysler, E.F.

461
00:30:59,870 --> 00:31:04,160
Hutton, and Condé Nast paid $300 a year in membership dues.

462
00:31:05,620 --> 00:31:09,820
The first floor of the Cloud Club held the lobby and private club rooms.

463
00:31:11,260 --> 00:31:14,140
There were a cocktail lounge.

464
00:31:14,141 --> 00:31:20,400
There was a room that had humidors that you could have your own to keep your cigars.

465
00:31:21,540 --> 00:31:27,800
There were also secret panels where members during the time of Prohibition could keep their illegal liquor.

466
00:31:30,520 --> 00:31:35,600
On the second floor of the Cloud Club, Chrysler had his private dining room.

467
00:31:36,360 --> 00:31:41,020
His private dining room is one of the coolest parts of the whole building

468
00:31:41,021 --> 00:31:45,860
because there's silver etched glass around his room

469
00:31:45,861 --> 00:31:47,760
depicting all the assembly line workers.

470
00:31:47,860 --> 00:31:49,360
And he was very proud of his workers.

471
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:55,600
By the end of May 1930, the Chrysler building

472
00:31:55,601 --> 00:31:58,020
had close to a 70% occupancy rate.

473
00:31:58,740 --> 00:32:00,780
It was an admirable figure given the Depression.

474
00:32:01,760 --> 00:32:05,400
But Walter P. Chrysler was about to receive a shock to his wallet

475
00:32:05,401 --> 00:32:08,440
that had nothing to do with tenants or the Depression.

476
00:32:08,441 --> 00:32:20,420
In the summer of 1930, Van Allen came to collect on his fee for designing the Chrysler building.

477
00:32:21,080 --> 00:32:26,880
And he wanted 6% of the building's cost, which was a standard in the industry.

478
00:32:27,500 --> 00:32:31,500
And Chrysler simply said, I'm not paying you that. There's no way.

479
00:32:32,660 --> 00:32:35,860
Van Allen wanted $745,000.

480
00:32:35,861 --> 00:32:39,440
There had been no written contract.

481
00:32:40,220 --> 00:32:44,100
Furthermore, Chrysler believed Van Allen had taken bribes from contractors.

482
00:32:44,800 --> 00:32:50,440
Now, there were rumors that Van Allen had been taking fees on the side from the contractors.

483
00:32:50,820 --> 00:32:55,820
It was never proven, and some of his former friends said that didn't really happen.

484
00:32:57,060 --> 00:33:04,760
Van Allen sued Chrysler and placed a lien against the building that would make it difficult for Chrysler to refinance or sell the skyscraper.

485
00:33:05,860 --> 00:33:08,500
There was a great deal of animosity between them

486
00:33:08,501 --> 00:33:09,400
at this point.

487
00:33:09,401 --> 00:33:12,340
Although they had worked together so wonderfully before,

488
00:33:12,341 --> 00:33:18,240
when it finally came down to the money, it just fell apart.

489
00:33:18,241 --> 00:33:21,700
And eventually, they settled.

490
00:33:21,701 --> 00:33:24,540
But by the very fact that Van Allen had

491
00:33:24,541 --> 00:33:28,100
sued the person who had given him his commission,

492
00:33:28,101 --> 00:33:30,620
he had put his career in jeopardy.

493
00:33:32,720 --> 00:33:35,500
Van Allen collected his fee, which some estimated

494
00:33:35,501 --> 00:33:38,620
may have been close to three quarters of a million dollars.

495
00:33:38,621 --> 00:33:41,260
But soon after, he fell into obscurity.

496
00:33:42,420 --> 00:33:45,160
In many respects, he never recovered.

497
00:33:45,161 --> 00:33:47,540
With the combination of the lawsuit

498
00:33:47,541 --> 00:33:50,940
and the depression hitting America,

499
00:33:50,941 --> 00:33:54,260
his great ambition as an architect was eclipsed.

500
00:33:56,760 --> 00:34:00,260
Van Allen died in 1954 at age 71.

501
00:34:02,060 --> 00:34:04,340
His work was not regarded as that important,

502
00:34:04,341 --> 00:34:06,740
And no one came forward to save it.

503
00:34:06,741 --> 00:34:09,500
It's not in any archive at a university.

504
00:34:09,501 --> 00:34:13,580
And so all of the stuff that would explain so much

505
00:34:13,581 --> 00:34:18,260
about these things that make us all curious is not there.

506
00:34:18,261 --> 00:34:21,380
It's unknown.

507
00:34:21,381 --> 00:34:25,400
Walter P. Chrysler died in 1940 at age 65,

508
00:34:25,401 --> 00:34:29,580
almost 15 years before Van Allen.

509
00:34:29,581 --> 00:34:34,080
In 1945, the observatory closed its doors to the public.

510
00:34:34,081 --> 00:34:36,720
it didn't generate enough revenue.

511
00:34:36,721 --> 00:34:39,960
30 years later in the 1970s, the Cloud Club

512
00:34:39,961 --> 00:34:43,780
fell into disuse and closed.

513
00:34:43,781 --> 00:34:46,760
And finally, Chrysler's private office

514
00:34:46,761 --> 00:34:52,420
was stripped and remodeled by a succession of tenants.

515
00:34:52,421 --> 00:34:54,840
New York's Chrysler Building still stands today

516
00:34:54,841 --> 00:34:57,580
as a private office building.

517
00:34:57,581 --> 00:35:01,800
But the Chrysler family sold its interest in 1953.

518
00:35:01,801 --> 00:35:05,900
In the ensuing years, the building fell on hard times.

519
00:35:05,901 --> 00:35:08,400
The 1970s were a terrible time for New York,

520
00:35:08,401 --> 00:35:10,720
and especially a terrible time for the Chrysler Building,

521
00:35:10,721 --> 00:35:13,980
which could be New York's symbol.

522
00:35:13,981 --> 00:35:16,100
The building went into foreclosure.

523
00:35:16,101 --> 00:35:19,180
People were walking away from it to the modern type of office

524
00:35:19,181 --> 00:35:23,160
building with big, clear floor spans.

525
00:35:23,161 --> 00:35:24,860
These buildings were like dinosaurs,

526
00:35:24,861 --> 00:35:27,060
and it seemed like there would be no future for them

527
00:35:27,061 --> 00:35:29,160
except as a sort of peculiar trophy.

528
00:35:31,800 --> 00:35:35,980
Then, the Tax Reform Act of 1976 was passed.

529
00:35:36,680 --> 00:35:40,980
It gave owners of commercial historic properties a tax write-off for restoring their buildings.

530
00:35:42,160 --> 00:35:48,240
It was actually one of the largest tax act projects in New York,

531
00:35:48,560 --> 00:35:52,940
which was a great preservation victory in the late 70s.

532
00:35:53,320 --> 00:35:57,680
And at that point, the Chrysler Building was looking pretty tatty.

533
00:35:57,960 --> 00:35:59,980
I can't imagine what the inside looked like.

534
00:35:59,981 --> 00:36:06,860
And so there was a major investment, and the building was totally refurbished at that point.

535
00:36:10,140 --> 00:36:14,660
The grand lobby and murals of the Chrysler Building were restored in 1979.

536
00:36:16,620 --> 00:36:20,580
The incandescent lights in the dome were also replaced with fluorescent lights,

537
00:36:21,240 --> 00:36:24,800
making the Chrysler an even brighter beacon on the New York City skyline.

538
00:36:24,801 --> 00:36:31,800
However, the biggest restoration of the Chrysler Building wouldn't come for another 20 years.

539
00:36:31,801 --> 00:36:40,800
In 1998, under new owners, Tishman Spire Properties, the Chrysler Building underwent a multi-million dollar facelift.

540
00:36:40,801 --> 00:36:47,800
They hired restoration specialists, LZA Technology, who conducted an evaluation of the building.

541
00:36:47,801 --> 00:36:54,140
Interestingly enough, throughout the building, from the ground to as high up on the spire

542
00:36:54,141 --> 00:37:00,700
as we've gotten, the original work, if it had been left alone, would perform much better

543
00:37:00,701 --> 00:37:02,800
than most of the repairs that had been done.

544
00:37:04,020 --> 00:37:05,780
Tishman's spire planned to do better.

545
00:37:06,840 --> 00:37:08,060
It started with the brickwork.

546
00:37:10,260 --> 00:37:14,380
The size of the brick used in the Chrysler building is known as a standard.

547
00:37:15,380 --> 00:37:18,300
Nowadays, bricks typically used are called modulars.

548
00:37:18,800 --> 00:37:21,360
Now, the difference is 3 eighths of an inch in length.

549
00:37:22,260 --> 00:37:25,160
None of those are off-the-shelf bricks that people use.

550
00:37:26,440 --> 00:37:30,160
LZA ordered a quarter of a million bricks from the original manufacturer.

551
00:37:32,900 --> 00:37:37,300
Believe it or not, an order of 250,000 brick is a small order.

552
00:37:38,600 --> 00:37:41,660
But not all of the brick repairs required brick replacement.

553
00:37:41,661 --> 00:37:46,120
equipment. Some needed what's known as repointing.

554
00:37:46,121 --> 00:37:51,060
Repointing a mortar joint is when you take out deteriorated mortar and place new mortar

555
00:37:51,061 --> 00:37:55,580
over it. You don't cut the entire depth of the mortar joint out, you cut back as far

556
00:37:55,581 --> 00:37:58,662
back as necessary to get to sound mortar.

557
00:37:58,663 --> 00:38:02,420
It's like filling a cavity on a tooth or repairing

558
00:38:02,421 --> 00:38:07,100
a piece of rotted wood. Through chemical analysis, LZA matched

559
00:38:07,101 --> 00:38:11,060
the original mortar and used it for repairs.

560
00:38:11,061 --> 00:38:15,160
Early 20th century buildings, the mortar used then

561
00:38:15,161 --> 00:38:17,260
is very similar to what we use today,

562
00:38:17,261 --> 00:38:19,240
but it had fewer things added to it.

563
00:38:19,241 --> 00:38:20,940
You didn't have latex.

564
00:38:20,941 --> 00:38:24,060
So it was really some basic straightforward cement,

565
00:38:24,061 --> 00:38:29,300
Portland cement, lime, hydrated lime, and sand.

566
00:38:29,301 --> 00:38:31,560
The restoration team also matched and replaced

567
00:38:31,561 --> 00:38:33,960
damaged marble and granite on the lower portions

568
00:38:33,961 --> 00:38:36,740
of the building.

569
00:38:36,741 --> 00:38:37,880
The windows were next.

570
00:38:38,500 --> 00:38:40,940
The Empire State Building had the same brand windows,

571
00:38:41,080 --> 00:38:43,400
and many had to be replaced during its restoration.

572
00:38:44,080 --> 00:38:47,560
But LZA was stunned to find that the windows on the Chrysler Building,

573
00:38:47,960 --> 00:38:50,600
all 3,800 of them, were just fine.

574
00:38:51,700 --> 00:38:55,680
Amazingly, when we did our first scaffold drops on the facade,

575
00:38:56,300 --> 00:38:58,500
we found that the windows were in very good condition.

576
00:38:58,740 --> 00:39:00,660
There were no known leaks around the windows.

577
00:39:02,000 --> 00:39:03,000
The difference?

578
00:39:03,500 --> 00:39:06,120
The Chrysler Building had galvanized steel windows.

579
00:39:06,121 --> 00:39:08,120
The Empire State Building did not.

580
00:39:08,121 --> 00:39:14,260
The window glass and the glazing, the sealant around between the glass and the steel was

581
00:39:14,261 --> 00:39:15,260
in very good shape.

582
00:39:15,261 --> 00:39:18,520
We didn't have to restore any of that.

583
00:39:18,521 --> 00:39:22,980
The windows instead received a fresh coat of paint and some resealing.

584
00:39:22,981 --> 00:39:26,720
The next project would be more challenging.

585
00:39:26,721 --> 00:39:33,180
They had been getting leaks into the spire and they asked LZA to look into it to see

586
00:39:33,181 --> 00:39:35,560
what might be done about them.

587
00:39:35,561 --> 00:39:39,060
The unusual job called for unusual methods.

588
00:39:42,060 --> 00:39:49,800
We knew of a company that uses rope climbing techniques to access difficult areas to do architectural investigations.

589
00:39:51,400 --> 00:39:55,340
A video camera documented the investigation on the Chrysler Spire.

590
00:39:55,960 --> 00:40:00,720
They found inside the stainless steel panels what they believed were the cause of the leaks.

591
00:40:00,721 --> 00:40:10,060
In the construction of the spire, they incorporated white lead paste within that seam to act as a sealant.

592
00:40:10,320 --> 00:40:14,560
Again, they didn't have the urethanes and the silicone sealants that we have today.

593
00:40:15,440 --> 00:40:20,020
But what happened was that white lead has eroded, deteriorated.

594
00:40:20,220 --> 00:40:22,260
For the most part, it doesn't exist anymore.

595
00:40:24,380 --> 00:40:28,860
They came up with a solution that would address the leaks without ripping up the spire.

596
00:40:28,861 --> 00:40:34,260
The restoration team covered the seams with a transparent sealant.

597
00:40:35,100 --> 00:40:39,820
So we could discreetly make repairs and not affect the entire facade

598
00:40:39,821 --> 00:40:42,440
and certainly not affect the way it looked from ground level.

599
00:40:44,100 --> 00:40:47,440
Restoration of the Chrysler building was completed in 2002.

600
00:40:48,580 --> 00:40:55,600
All the craftwork done in 1929 was the result of techniques perfected long before the skyscraper was a vision.

601
00:40:55,601 --> 00:41:00,600
If you look at the steel frame, the facades, the cranes that

602
00:41:00,601 --> 00:41:04,600
were used for construction, the logistics of getting men up

603
00:41:04,601 --> 00:41:07,600
in the elevators, all of these are very normal things.

604
00:41:07,601 --> 00:41:10,600
And I think that it's proof of how good those techniques were,

605
00:41:10,601 --> 00:41:13,600
that they were developed for much smaller, shorter buildings,

606
00:41:13,601 --> 00:41:16,600
but they worked just fine in building this extremely tall

607
00:41:16,601 --> 00:41:19,600
and extremely complex building.

608
00:41:19,601 --> 00:41:22,600
Today, the Chrysler Building has found its way back

609
00:41:22,601 --> 00:41:24,940
its way back into the hearts of New Yorkers.

610
00:41:25,660 --> 00:41:28,540
I think most New Yorkers love the building,

611
00:41:28,940 --> 00:41:33,020
partly because for many years we had big glass boxes.

612
00:41:33,740 --> 00:41:35,800
But most people want more fun in the city.

613
00:41:36,440 --> 00:41:39,240
It's something that makes Midtown special

614
00:41:39,241 --> 00:41:42,500
by giving it a certain elegance,

615
00:41:42,501 --> 00:41:45,900
a certain sparkle, especially at night.

616
00:41:47,720 --> 00:41:51,240
I think it's something that is an example of American modernism.

617
00:41:51,241 --> 00:41:54,760
You can say it was influenced, if you wish, by Art Deco,

618
00:41:55,000 --> 00:41:57,840
but it isn't Art Deco because that would make it French.

619
00:41:57,880 --> 00:41:58,760
It's not French.

620
00:41:58,920 --> 00:42:01,500
It's of its own time and of its own place.

621
00:42:01,980 --> 00:42:02,940
That's New York.

622
00:42:05,240 --> 00:42:06,560
The Chrysler Building.

623
00:42:07,660 --> 00:42:10,800
Its sober and stately base gives way to the fanciful

624
00:42:10,801 --> 00:42:15,420
as each setback is topped with sculpture more astonishing and unexpected

625
00:42:15,421 --> 00:42:17,020
than the one before it.

626
00:42:17,880 --> 00:42:20,400
The tower, its own work of art,

627
00:42:20,401 --> 00:42:23,340
reflects the light and mood of the sky above.

628
00:42:23,860 --> 00:42:26,580
It is the work of craftsmen and visionaries

629
00:42:26,581 --> 00:42:29,140
who, for an extraordinary moment in time,

630
00:42:29,920 --> 00:42:33,600
were able to embody the dreams and aspirations of their generation

631
00:42:33,601 --> 00:42:36,620
in one glorious skyscraper.

